# Plan: implement `rb_insert` (+ `rb_find`, `rb_size` for verifiability)

## Context
`src/rbtree.c` has real `rb_create` (single `malloc`, `root=NULL`,
NULL-as-black convention, explicit `parent` pointers — from the prior
slice). Everything else is still a stub. This slice implements the actual
M1 algorithm: BST insert + red-black insert-fixup (rotations + recoloring).

Bundled in per your choice: `rb_find` and `rb_size`. Both are trivial,
non-algorithmic reads (plain BST search; return a stored counter) — neither
touches colors, rotations, or fixup — but without them the demo has no way
to observe whether `rb_insert` actually worked (its own `rb_find` calls
were stubbed to always return NULL). `rb_foreach` and `rb_validate` stay
stubs, deferred to their own slices — `rb_validate` in particular is
algorithmically substantial enough (5 invariants) to deserve its own plan.

New `#include <string.h>` needed in `src/rbtree.c` (`strcmp`, `strlen`,
`memcpy`). Assumption carried from the frozen header: `t` and `key` are
required non-NULL, same as the rest of the API — only `rb_destroy` is
documented NULL-safe.

## Helpers (all `static`, internal to `src/rbtree.c`)

```c
static rb_color_t node_color(const rb_node_t *n) {
    return n == NULL ? RB_BLACK : n->color;   /* NULL reads as black */
}

static void rotate_left(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *x) {
    rb_node_t *y = x->right;
    x->right = y->left;
    if (y->left != NULL) y->left->parent = x;
    y->parent = x->parent;
    if (x->parent == NULL)          t->root = y;
    else if (x == x->parent->left)  x->parent->left = y;
    else                             x->parent->right = y;
    y->left = x;
    x->parent = y;
}

static void rotate_right(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *x) {
    /* mirror of rotate_left: x->left, y->right, y->right->parent, etc. */
}

static void insert_fixup(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *z) {
    while (node_color(z->parent) == RB_RED) {
        rb_node_t *gp = z->parent->parent; /* parent red => parent isn't
                                               root => gp exists */
        if (z->parent == gp->left) {
            rb_node_t *uncle = gp->right;
            if (node_color(uncle) == RB_RED) {
                z->parent->color = RB_BLACK;
                uncle->color = RB_BLACK;
                gp->color = RB_RED;
                z = gp;
            } else {
                if (z == z->parent->right) {
                    z = z->parent;
                    rotate_left(t, z);
                }
                z->parent->color = RB_BLACK;
                gp->color = RB_RED;
                rotate_right(t, gp);
            }
        } else {
            /* mirror: uncle = gp->left, rotate_right/rotate_left swapped */
        }
    }
    t->root->color = RB_BLACK;
}
```
`rotate_left`/`rotate_right`/`insert_fixup` allocate and free nothing —
pure pointer rewiring and recoloring.

### Insert-fixup cases (enumerated per CLAUDE.md's rigor rule)
- **Loop doesn't run** (`z->parent` black, or `z` is root/`parent == NULL`
  since `node_color(NULL) == BLACK`): nothing to do. Covers the
  first-ever insert uniformly — no special-casing needed.
- **Case 1, uncle red:** recolor parent+uncle black, grandparent red,
  move `z` up to grandparent, loop continues (may repeat further up).
- **Case 2, uncle black/NULL, `z` is the "inner" child (triangle):**
  rotate at `z->parent` to convert into Case 3 (line shape), then fall
  through.
- **Case 3, uncle black/NULL, `z` is the "outer" child (line):** recolor
  parent black, grandparent red, rotate at grandparent. Parent (now
  black) ends the loop.
- Mirror of all three when `z->parent` is the grandparent's *right*
  child (uncle = `gp->left`, rotations swapped).
- **Final line, unconditional:** `t->root->color = RB_BLACK` — restores
  root-is-black after any of the above, and is what makes the very first
  insert (loop never ran) end up black too.

## `rb_insert`
```c
int rb_insert(rbtree_t *t, const char *key, void *value) {
    rb_node_t *cur = t->root;
    rb_node_t *parent = NULL;
    int cmp = 0;

    while (cur != NULL) {
        cmp = strcmp(key, cur->key);
        if (cmp == 0) {
            if (t->value_free != NULL) {
                t->value_free(cur->value);
            }
            cur->value = value;
            return 0;
        }
        parent = cur;
        cur = (cmp < 0) ? cur->left : cur->right;
    }

    rb_node_t *n = malloc(sizeof(*n));
    if (n == NULL) {
        return -1;
    }
    size_t klen = strlen(key) + 1;
    char *kcopy = malloc(klen);
    if (kcopy == NULL) {
        free(n);
        return -1;
    }
    memcpy(kcopy, key, klen);

    n->key = kcopy;
    n->value = value;
    n->color = RB_RED;
    n->left = NULL;
    n->right = NULL;
    n->parent = parent;

    if (parent == NULL)      t->root = n;
    else if (cmp < 0)        parent->left = n;
    else                     parent->right = n;
    t->size++;

    insert_fixup(t, n);
    return 0;
}
```

### Where each path frees/owns memory (per CLAUDE.md's requirement)
- **Overwrite path** (`cmp == 0`): no allocation. Frees the *old* value
  via `value_free` (only if non-NULL), then installs the caller's new
  `value` — new value's ownership transfers to the tree, matching the
  header's "overwriting frees the old value via `value_free`" contract.
- **`malloc(sizeof(*n))` fails:** return -1 immediately. Nothing
  allocated yet; `value` untouched — caller retains ownership, tree
  unchanged. Matches contract.
- **`malloc(klen)` fails:** `free(n)` (the just-allocated node, goto-
  cleanup style per CLAUDE.md, just inlined since there's only one prior
  allocation to unwind), return -1. `value` still untouched/caller-owned.
  Tree unchanged (node was never linked in).
- **Full success:** node + key copy are now tree-owned (freed later only
  by `rb_delete`/`rb_destroy`); `value` ownership transfers to the tree
  (released later via `value_free` on overwrite/delete/destroy).

## `rb_find` / `rb_size`
```c
void *rb_find(const rbtree_t *t, const char *key) {
    rb_node_t *cur = t->root;
    while (cur != NULL) {
        int cmp = strcmp(key, cur->key);
        if (cmp == 0) return cur->value;
        cur = (cmp < 0) ? cur->left : cur->right;
    }
    return NULL;
}

size_t rb_size(const rbtree_t *t) {
    return t->size;
}
```
Both read-only, no allocation, no ownership implications.

## Explicitly out of scope for this slice
- `rb_delete`, `rb_foreach`, `rb_validate`, `rb_destroy` stay stubs.
- `rb_validate` still stub-returns 0 ("valid") — so `confirm_tree_is_valid`
  in the demo will keep printing "valid" regardless of actual tree shape;
  that's a known, expected gap until `rb_validate`'s own slice, not a
  signal to trust yet.
- `rb_foreach` still stub (no-op) — `print_all_entries_in_order` in the
  demo will print its header line but no entries, even though nodes now
  really exist. `rb_find`/`rb_size` are the only observable signal this
  slice.

## Verification
- `module load gcc/15.2.0 && make demo` — clean build, no warnings.
- `./build/demo` — `build_sample_tree()` should now fully succeed (no
  "rb_insert failed" line); `confirm_lookup_works` should report all 5
  sample keys "found" and "nonexistent" "correctly absent";
  `confirm_tree_is_valid` will print a stub "valid" regardless (see
  above — not real confirmation yet).
- Run the demo binary directly under ASan/UBSan and valgrind (ad hoc —
  `make asan`/`make memcheck` still can't run since `tests/` doesn't
  exist yet) to confirm the insert/overwrite/find paths are leak-free and
  UB-free: e.g. `gcc -std=c23 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fsanitize=address,undefined -Iinclude src/rbtree.c demo/main.c -o build/demo_asan && ./build/demo_asan`, and separately `valgrind --leak-check=full ./build/demo` (the latter will still report the tree itself as "still reachable" since `rb_destroy` is a no-op stub — expected, not a regression from this slice).

