# Plan: implement `rb_create` (and the internal node/tree struct it needs)

## Context
`src/rbtree.c` currently has all 8 API functions as stubs (`rb_create`
returns `NULL` unconditionally). This is the first real-logic slice of M1.
`rb_create` is the natural starting point because every other function
depends on the internal layout of `struct rbtree` (opaque per
`include/rbtree.h`) and its node type — so this slice also defines that
layout, even though only `rb_create`'s body gets real logic. The other 7
functions stay stubs, touched one at a time in later slices, per CLAUDE.md's
"implement in small slices" rule.

User requirement: nodes carry an explicit `parent` pointer.
User's NIL-representation choice (asked directly, since it's foundational
and hard to change later): **plain `NULL` treated as black**, not a shared
sentinel node. Single allocation in `rb_create`, simpler than the sentinel's
2-allocation + special-cased-destroy tradeoff. The cost lands later, in M2's
delete-fixup, which will need to thread the relevant parent through function
arguments explicitly instead of reading it off a sentinel's `->parent` — a
concern for that slice's own plan, not this one.

## Internal types (new, in `src/rbtree.c` only — `include/rbtree.h` untouched)

```c
typedef enum { RB_RED, RB_BLACK } rb_color_t;

typedef struct rb_node {
    char            *key;      /* owned copy */
    void            *value;    /* owned iff value_free != NULL, per contract */
    rb_color_t       color;
    struct rb_node  *parent;   /* NULL at root */
    struct rb_node  *left;
    struct rb_node  *right;
} rb_node_t;

struct rbtree {
    rb_node_t        *root;    /* NULL = empty tree */
    size_t             size;
    rb_value_free_fn   value_free;
};
```
`NULL` children/root are treated as black wherever color is read (that
logic belongs to `rb_validate`/insert-fixup/delete-fixup, not `rb_create`;
noted here only so the convention is on record for those later slices).

## `rb_create` body
Single allocation, so a plain NULL-check-and-return is enough — CLAUDE.md's
goto-cleanup pattern is reserved for *multi*-allocation functions, and this
isn't one:

```c
rbtree_t *rb_create(rb_value_free_fn value_free) {
    rbtree_t *t = malloc(sizeof(*t));
    if (t == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }
    t->root = NULL;
    t->size = 0;
    t->value_free = value_free;
    return t;
}
```

## Explicitly out of scope for this slice
- `rb_insert`, `rb_find`, `rb_delete`, `rb_size`, `rb_foreach`,
  `rb_validate`, `rb_destroy` stay stubs (unchanged bodies). `rb_size`
  could trivially return `t->size` now that the field exists, but that's a
  second function touched in one diff — deferred to its own slice.
- Any color/black-height logic (nothing to balance with zero or one node).
- Sentinel-node representation (declined above).

## Verification
- `module load gcc/15.2.0 && make demo` — clean build, no warnings.
- `./build/demo` — `build_sample_tree()`'s `rb_create(free)` call now
  succeeds (no more "rb_create failed" line); it will still report
  `rb_insert(banana) failed` and unwind via the still-stub `rb_destroy`,
  since insert isn't implemented yet — expected for this slice.
- Full leak-free confirmation of the `rb_create`/`rb_destroy` pair (via
  `make asan`/`make memcheck` on the real test suite) follows once
  `rb_destroy` has a real body and `tests/test_rbtree.c` exists — not
  meaningful yet since `rb_destroy` is still a no-op stub.

